  ],
  "text": "Sleep is not one state. You cycle through four of them, about five times a night. It feels like a switch. Off, then on again eight hours later. The first stage is barely sleep. It is the drift where a noise still wakes you. Then you drop into deep slow wave sleep, and this is where physical repair happens. It is also where the day gets filed. Wake someone here and they are useless for minutes. Then something strange. Your brain lights up like you are awake, and your body goes limp. That is REM. It is when you dream, and the paralysis is what stops you acting it out. Each full cycle takes about ninety minutes, and the mix shifts as the night goes on. Deep sleep is loaded into the first half. REM grows towards morning. So cutting the last two hours does not trim your sleep evenly. It takes your dreaming. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
